From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] win32u, server: the display scale changes while programs run

Settings > Display > Scale wrote LogPixels and told every window
(WM_SETTINGCHANGE "WindowMetrics"), but Wine read the scale once, when a
program started: programs already running stayed at the old scale until
the user signed out (David 2026-10-05: "it'd be nice if we could do so
while logged in without having to restart the session"; a Surface Pro 7
at 175%). Wine now does what Windows does when the scale changes:

- The monitors take the new DPI. A top-level window told of a settings
  change has its process read LogPixels again; when it differs from the
  monitors' DPI, that is written to their registry records and the monitor
  serial tells every process to read them again (sync_monitor_dpi). The
  monitors' DPI is always the user's LogPixels now (or the scale handed to
  the process in SG_LOGPIXELS, 0882), not the system DPI of whichever
  process enumerated the displays; a virtual desktop's detached source has
  the others' DPI, not the reading process's.
- A per-monitor aware window gets WM_DPICHANGED with the new DPI and the
  suggested rectangle: its client area scaled, its frame the new DPI's
  (v2) or as it is (v1), kept on the work area. A v2 window's children get
  WM_DPICHANGED_BEFOREPARENT (bottom up) before it and
  WM_DPICHANGED_AFTERPARENT (top down) after; it may size itself first
  (WM_GETDPISCALEDSIZE). Its frame is laid out again.
- A window that is not (DPI unaware, aware of the system DPI) keeps its
  own DPI -- as on Windows, a running program's system DPI does not change
  -- and Wine scales it to the monitor's new one (its filtered scaled
  surface). Programs started afterwards get the new scale as their system
  DPI.
- A layered window drawn with UpdateLayeredWindow is handed its picture
  again at the new scale, as the program would (it is not asked to paint).
- A maximized window fills the new work area.
- Per-monitor v2 is kept: the server made every per-monitor aware
  top-level window v1, so v2 programs had neither the children's
  notifications nor a frame at their DPI. A v2 window's frame -- title bar,
  borders, menu bar, caption buttons -- is now laid out, drawn and
  hit-tested at the window's DPI, as Windows scales it (the system metrics
  are the window's DPI's while DefWindowProc handles the frame; a message
  sent from there to a program has them as usual).

At 100% on a 1080p screen nothing changes.

Test: test/dpilive-gate.sh with test/dpilive-probe.c (mutants
SG_MUTANT_DPI_LIVE in sysparams.c, SG_MUTANT_DPI_V2_FRAME in defwnd.c,
SG_MUTANT_DPI_V1_ONLY in server/window.c).
---
--- a/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/defwnd.c
@@ -3086,8 +3086,50 @@
 }
 
 
+static LRESULT default_window_proc_dpi( HWND hwnd, UINT msg, WPARAM wparam, LPARAM lparam, BOOL ansi );
+
+/* Stained Glass OS: a per-monitor v2 window's frame -- its title bar,
+ * borders, menu bar, caption buttons -- at the window's DPI, as Windows
+ * scales it (v1 and others keep the process's system DPI). The system
+ * metrics are the window's DPI's while DefWindowProc lays out, draws and
+ * hit-tests the frame; a message sent from there to a program's window
+ * procedure has them as usual again (call_window_proc). */
 LRESULT default_window_proc( HWND hwnd, UINT msg, WPARAM wparam, LPARAM lparam, BOOL ansi )
 {
+    UINT context, prev, dpi;
+    LRESULT ret;
+
+    switch (msg)
+    {
+    case WM_NCCALCSIZE:
+    case WM_NCPAINT:
+    case WM_NCACTIVATE:
+    case WM_NCHITTEST:
+    case WM_NCMOUSEMOVE:
+    case WM_NCMOUSELEAVE:
+    case WM_SETTEXT:
+    case WM_GETMINMAXINFO:
+    case WM_PRINT:
+        context = get_window_dpi_awareness_context( hwnd );
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_DPI_V2_FRAME
+        if (NTUSER_DPI_CONTEXT_IS_MONITOR_AWARE( context ) && NTUSER_DPI_CONTEXT_GET_VERSION( context ) >= 2 &&
+            (dpi = get_dpi_for_window( hwnd )))
+        {
+            prev = set_thread_metrics_dpi( dpi );
+            ret = default_window_proc_dpi( hwnd, msg, wparam, lparam, ansi );
+            set_thread_metrics_dpi( prev );
+            return ret;
+        }
+#else
+        (void)dpi; (void)prev; (void)ret;
+#endif
+        break;
+    }
+    return default_window_proc_dpi( hwnd, msg, wparam, lparam, ansi );
+}
+
+static LRESULT default_window_proc_dpi( HWND hwnd, UINT msg, WPARAM wparam, LPARAM lparam, BOOL ansi )
+{
     LRESULT result = 0;
 
     switch (msg)
